{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent underprediction in rising regimes: when the last 5–10 days show an upswing, add a positive level shift to the whole path (model sits ~+1–3 EUR/t too low early/mid-horizon in Examples 1–3).",
    "Trend/acceleration lag: the forecast reacts too slowly to sharp upward moves; apply a stronger near-term trend component (boost days 1–15 more than days 16–30 when momentum is positive).",
    "Over-optimism at longer horizons after strong runs: beyond ~day 20 the model can overshoot/hold prices too high relative to realized pullbacks (notably Example 2 late-horizon); taper the forecast back toward a mean-reversion anchor after day ~15–20.",
    "Horizon-dependent bias: use a two-piece adjustment—(i) add +1–2 EUR/t for days 1–15 in bullish momentum, (ii) apply a mild negative drift or mean-reversion shrink for days 16–30 to avoid late-horizon overhang.",
    "Volatility underestimation around spikes: realized peaks/troughs are more extreme than forecast; widen amplitude by scaling deviations from the starting level (especially in the first half of the horizon) rather than only shifting the level."
  ]
}
